Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Your body is sending a clear signal — it's time to rest and recover.",
  "summary": "This week's data tells a consistent story: every single muscle group shows a decline in strength index compared to last period, your joints are aching, you feel run down, and your sessions are getting worse rather than better. These are classic signs of accumulated fatigue that has outpaced your recovery. At 28 with a healthy BMI, your baseline capacity is strong, but no amount of youth or fitness protects against pushing through accumulated overreaching. The good news is that this is very fixable. A deliberate deload this coming period will allow your nervous system and connective tissue to catch up, and you should come back stronger and ready to build again.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "status": "regressing",
    "notes": "Every muscle group regressed this period without exception. Chest dropped from 105 to 99, back from 104 to 98, legs from 104 to 97, shoulders from 103 to 99, biceps from 101 to 100, and triceps from 102 to 100. This is a universal, multi-group regression — not a one-off bad session or a single lagging lift. The breadth and consistency of these declines strongly indicate systemic fatigue rather than a technique or programming issue with any specific exercise."
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Legs",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Legs represent only 22.4% of total volume split, which sits noticeably below chest (26.8%) and back (28%). For a muscle building goal, legs typically warrant volume closer to or on par with the upper body prime movers. This is worth addressing once recovery is restored."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Biceps",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Biceps account for just 4% of total volume, the lowest of any group logged. While biceps do receive indirect stimulus from back work, this proportion is low even accounting for that overlap and may limit arm development over time."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": true,
  "deloadReason": "All six tracked muscle groups declined in strength index this period, and the user explicitly reports joint aches, systemic fatigue, and worsening session quality. These combined subjective and objective signals are strong indicators of accumulated overreaching. A deload — reducing volume to roughly 50% of current sets and keeping loads light and comfortable — is the appropriate response to allow full recovery before resuming progressive loading.",
  "actionItems": [
    "Deload for the full coming period: cut total sets to roughly half your usual volume (aim for around 25 to 30 sets total) and reduce loads to around 50 to 60% of your recent working weights. Move, but do not push.",
    "Prioritise sleep and nutrition this week — both are primary recovery levers. Aim for at least 7 to 9 hours of sleep per night and ensure you are eating at or slightly above maintenance calories with adequate protein (around 1.6 to 2g per kg of bodyweight).",
    "Once you return from the deload, gradually rebuild leg volume to bring it closer to 25 to 28% of your total split, in line with chest and back, to better support your muscle building goal.",
    "After the deload, consider adding one or two sets of direct biceps work per session to bring that muscle group's volume share up from its current 4% to something closer to 6 to 8% of total volume."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Universal strength regression across all muscle groups combined with reported joint pain and systemic fatigue confirmed overreaching; a full deload was recommended and recovery markers should be assessed at the start of the next period before resuming progressive loading."
}
